1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,811
What does that HSPF2 number on a heat pump actually mean?

2
00:00:03,931 --> 00:00:10,141
It's how many B T U of heat you get for each watt-hour of electricity, over a whole heating season.

3
00:00:10,261 --> 00:00:15,073
An electric heater turns one watt-hour into three point four one two B T U.

4
00:00:15,193 --> 00:00:16,250
That's the baseline.

5
00:00:16,370 --> 00:00:19,701
The federal minimum for a new heat pump is seven point five.

6
00:00:19,821 --> 00:00:25,648
Divide by three point four one two, and it gives about two point two times the heat of a heater,

7
00:00:25,768 --> 00:00:28,331
for about forty-five percent of the electricity.

8
00:00:28,451 --> 00:00:31,598
A model rated nine gives about two point six four,

9
00:00:31,718 --> 00:00:33,113
about thirty-eight percent.

10
00:00:33,233 --> 00:00:40,446
In a typical Minnesota home, that's about one thousand sixty-five dollars a year versus about eight hundred eighty-eight.

11
00:00:40,566 --> 00:00:42,997
About one hundred seventy-eight dollars saved.

12
00:00:43,117 --> 00:00:48,882
So next time you see HSPF2 on a heat pump, you'll know what that number actually means…
